{"moveId":"con-09-dogmatism-on-both-sides","type":"bias","label":"Subjective validation","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"O’Connor's personal acquaintances provide anecdotal evidence, but he does not treat the conclusion as accurate because it feels meaningful or confirms a private experience. The assessment flags inadequate prevalence evidence, which does not exactly instantiate subjective validation."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"subjective-validation","label":"Subjective validation","definition":"Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-09-dogmatism-on-both-sides","side":"con","sectionTitle":"Epistemic openness and dogmatism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"O’Connor replies that reasoned inquiry and unreflective commitment occur among both atheists and Christians, so sophisticated apologetics does not establish greater Christian openness.","lockedProposition":"Reasoned inquiry and unreflective commitment occur on both sides, so apologetics does not establish greater Christian openness.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"I know more atheists who were just atheists because yeah I've never seen any evidence whatever haven't given it much thought yeah then I know atheists who've sort of reasoned their way into that conclusion I think it happens on both sides in other words but you're","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: O’Connor answers with symmetrical calibration, candidly conceding unreflective atheists while distinguishing them from people who reason toward nonbelief and extending that distinction fairly across communities. Principal limitation: His personal acquaintance offers anecdotal illustration rather than comparative evidence about prevalence, causes, or degrees of closure among either Christians or atheists. Live burden: Hirsi Ali must justify her claim of greater activist-atheist certainty, while O’Connor need only defeat the asymmetry rather than prove equal distributions of dogmatism."}}
{"moveId":"pro-16-concedes-some-christian-certainty","type":"bias","label":"Subjective validation","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"After conceding Christian certainty, Hirsi Ali presents the wonderful feeling of having an answer and a loving conversational relationship with God as personal assurance, although that resonance cannot establish that the perceived divine interlocutor exists."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Hirsi Ali presents the personally wonderful experience of prayer and a loving relationship with God as her assurance that she has the answer. The assessment notes that this experiential assurance does not establish that the perceived divine interlocutor exists."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"subjective-validation","label":"Subjective validation","definition":"Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-16-concedes-some-christian-certainty","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Epistemic openness and dogmatism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali concedes that many Christians claim closed certainty, while grounding her own assurance in a loving, conversational, and prayerful relationship with God.","lockedProposition":"She concedes that many Christians claim certainty, while describing a loving, prayerful relationship with God as personal assurance.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"that's the case for a lot of Christians and I have to tell you on a personal level it is wonderful to have the answer to have this relationship with God a god who's loving and whom I talk to and pray","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali makes a substantial and well-calibrated concession that O’Connor’s description applies to many Christians, preventing her earlier criticism from becoming a categorical double standard. Principal limitation: Her loving prayer relationship explains the experiential character of assurance but does not show that the perceived interlocutor exists or that her confidence remains open to correction. Live burden: She must distinguish personally stabilizing trust from inquiry-ending certainty and provide procedures by which contrary evidence could revise her conclusion."}}
{"moveId":"con-10-christian-certainty-counterexample","type":"bias","label":"Confirmation bias","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The hypothetical Christian posture illustrates how scriptural certainty could close inquiry, but the move offers no evidence that O’Connor sought only confirming examples or discounted contrary ones. Its limitation is unproved frequency and representativeness, not demonstrated confirmation bias."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"confirmation-bias","label":"Confirmation bias","definition":"Seeking, interpreting, or remembering evidence in ways that favor an existing view while discounting contrary information.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/confirmation-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-10-christian-certainty-counterexample","side":"con","sectionTitle":"Epistemic openness and dogmatism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"O’Connor answers that Christians can likewise treat scripture as a final answer to ultimate questions and therefore see no need for further inquiry.","lockedProposition":"Christian scriptural certainty can close inquiry in the same way attributed to activist atheism.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"mean people say that that's what the what the Christians say they say I have my answer in this book and I'm I'm a humble Christian who has the answer to the to the biggest question of the universe and knows the truth and there's no need for further inquiry because we have we have the Scripture it tells us tells us the way things are say that","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: O’Connor applies Hirsi Ali’s closure mechanism symmetrically, showing how possessing a supposedly final scriptural answer can terminate inquiry just as confidently as activist atheism might. Principal limitation: The recognizable posture is presented hypothetically, without evidence establishing its frequency or distinguishing trust in scripture from total refusal to investigate interpretation and counterevidence. Live burden: Hirsi Ali must explain why her own assurance remains corrigible or retreat from comparative claims, while O’Connor must avoid generalizing this counterexample to all Christians."}}
{"moveId":"assurance-prompts-inquiry","type":"bias","label":"Confirmation bias","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The move says prior assurance intensifies efforts to prove and find the presumed something, explicitly orienting inquiry toward confirmation while supplying no procedure for seeking disconfirmation or abandoning the initial belief if contrary evidence emerges."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The proposed inquiry begins by taking the existence claim as given and becoming involved in proving it, while no comparable role is assigned to disconfirmation. The assessment expressly identifies the unresolved divide between truth-seeking and confirmation-seeking."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"confirmation-bias","label":"Confirmation bias","definition":"Seeking, interpreting, or remembering evidence in ways that favor an existing view while discounting contrary information.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/confirmation-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"assurance-prompts-inquiry","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Epistemic openness and dogmatism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali argues that accepting some transcendent reality can intensify curiosity, motivating efforts to prove, investigate, and identify what that reality is.","lockedProposition":"Assurance that something exists can increase intellectual efforts to prove, investigate, and identify it, rather than ending inquiry.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"level the when you when you take the position there is something I think you are then much more involved mentally in proving or even finding out the curiosity is very high it's finding out well if there's something what is it then and and where can I find it so I think that that opens up just a whole lot more","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali directly rejects the claim that prior belief necessarily ends inquiry by offering a plausible motivational pathway from provisional assurance to intensified investigation and curiosity. Principal limitation: Her language alternates between proving a presupposed result and finding out what is true, without addressing how confirmation-seeking will be separated from genuine attempts at disconfirmation. Live burden: She must specify standards capable of revising or abandoning the initial belief, because greater investigative activity does not by itself guarantee truth-sensitive inquiry."}}
{"moveId":"pro-17-experience-reading-and-miracles","type":"bias","label":"Belief bias","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"She chooses belief despite supplying no authentication of the miracle reports, but this excerpt does not say she judges an argument valid because its conclusion feels believable; the assessment identifies absent warrant and voluntarism rather than that defined bias."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Hirsi Ali chooses belief without stating an evidential argument, but the move does not evaluate an argument as sound because its conclusion feels believable. The assessment identifies bare voluntarism and missing warrant, which are not sufficient to establish belief bias."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"belief-bias","label":"Belief bias","definition":"Judging an argument by whether its conclusion feels believable instead of evaluating whether the conclusion follows from the premises.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/belief-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-17-experience-reading-and-miracles","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Choice, experience, and resurrection","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali acknowledges the difficulty of ancient miracle accounts but treats rarity as inherent to miracles and nevertheless chooses to believe them.","lockedProposition":"She chooses belief while acknowledging that ancient miracle accounts are extraordinary and must be interpreted from a modern standpoint.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"can't you know the stories that are told um again this is these are fast Century accounts of things that were happening and I'm using my 21st century brain to digest it and to understand it but if we say Miracles are really extraordinary things cuz if they happened all the time they wouldn't be Miracles um then I choose to believe and","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali openly acknowledges both the antiquity of the accounts and the extraordinary character of miracles, avoiding any pretense that resurrection belief resembles ordinary historical assent. Principal limitation: Defining miracles as rare explains why they would be extraordinary but supplies no reason that these reports are authentic, correctly interpreted, or preferable to natural alternatives. Live burden: O’Connor’s voluntarism objection requires an account of what produced conviction beyond decision, including historical evidence or experiential warrant capable of discriminating Christianity from rival traditions."}}
{"moveId":"pro-18-fruits-as-empirical-testament","type":"bias","label":"Base-rate neglect","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The claimed empirical testament focuses on selected Christian communities' happiness, prosperity, and connectedness while omitting comparison classes, counterexamples, historical background rates, and the mixed religious and secular inheritance of prosperous societies."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The comparison omits measurements and a defined comparison class, but no vivid specific case is weighed against an identifiable general background rate. The exact base-rate-neglect definition therefore is not materially instantiated by this broad unsupported assertion."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"base-rate-neglect","label":"Base-rate neglect","definition":"Underweighting general background rates or prior probabilities while focusing too heavily on a vivid specific case or local detail.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/base-rate-neglect/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-18-fruits-as-empirical-testament","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Choice, experience, and resurrection","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali replies that Christian-shaped communities’ greater happiness, prosperity, and connectedness provide observable social evidence, cumulatively supporting faith beyond its personal benefits.","lockedProposition":"Christian communities' comparative happiness, prosperity, and connectedness function for her as observable social support for the faith.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"that it's watching um and and for me this is empir an empirical Testament is the the communities the societies that build their lives on that faith and live by those laws tend to be happier more prosperous more connected","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali attempts to supplement private experience with publicly observable outcomes, identifying happiness, prosperity, and social connection as possible evidence for Christianity’s practical explanatory power. Principal limitation: The comparison class, measurements, causal mechanism, historical period, counterexamples, and alternative causes are all unspecified, while prosperous societies frequently reflect mixed religious and secular inheritances. Live burden: Even a demonstrated social advantage would establish utility more readily than resurrection or divine revelation, so she must defend both the empirical comparison and its truth-directed warrant."}}
{"moveId":"pro-18-fruits-as-empirical-testament","type":"bias","label":"Confirmation bias","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The move interprets selected claims of happiness, prosperity, and connection as empirical support for Christianity while leaving counterexamples and mixed religious and secular causes out of view. That selective interpretation is expressly reflected in the assessment."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"confirmation-bias","label":"Confirmation bias","definition":"Seeking, interpreting, or remembering evidence in ways that favor an existing view while discounting contrary information.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/confirmation-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-18-fruits-as-empirical-testament","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Choice, experience, and resurrection","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali replies that Christian-shaped communities’ greater happiness, prosperity, and connectedness provide observable social evidence, cumulatively supporting faith beyond its personal benefits.","lockedProposition":"Christian communities' comparative happiness, prosperity, and connectedness function for her as observable social support for the faith.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"that it's watching um and and for me this is empir an empirical Testament is the the communities the societies that build their lives on that faith and live by those laws tend to be happier more prosperous more connected","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali attempts to supplement private experience with publicly observable outcomes, identifying happiness, prosperity, and social connection as possible evidence for Christianity’s practical explanatory power. Principal limitation: The comparison class, measurements, causal mechanism, historical period, counterexamples, and alternative causes are all unspecified, while prosperous societies frequently reflect mixed religious and secular inheritances. Live burden: Even a demonstrated social advantage would establish utility more readily than resurrection or divine revelation, so she must defend both the empirical comparison and its truth-directed warrant."}}
{"moveId":"pro-19-explicit-resurrection-belief","type":"bias","label":"Belief bias","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The move passes from the resurrection story's redemptive meaning—God dying for sins so people are forgiven and can live—to factual acceptance without an evidential inference, allowing the appealing conclusion to substitute for whether the occurrence follows from evidence."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The move accepts the resurrection story while emphasizing its redemptive meaning—sin, forgiveness, and renewed life—without an inference supporting its occurrence. The assessment specifically distinguishes the story's meaning from evidence that the event happened."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"belief-bias","label":"Belief bias","definition":"Judging an argument by whether its conclusion feels believable instead of evaluating whether the conclusion follows from the premises.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/belief-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-19-explicit-resurrection-belief","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Choice, experience, and resurrection","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali directly affirms belief in the resurrection and in the redemptive account of God dying so humanity may receive forgiveness and life.","lockedProposition":"She chooses to believe the resurrection and the account of God dying for human sins.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"choose to believe in the resurrection yeah and also the the story itself and what it tells us um a God who died for our sins so that we are forgiven so that we can live and I","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali answers O’Connor’s clarification unambiguously, affirming both the physical resurrection’s doctrinal center and its redemptive meaning concerning sin, forgiveness, and renewed life. Principal limitation: The reply reiterates the Christian story and her decision to accept it without supplying historical evidence, explanatory comparison, or a warrant connecting its meaning to its occurrence. Live burden: Having established the exact proposition at issue, she must explain why choosing belief is rationally justified despite O’Connor’s distinction between desired commitment and factual conviction."}}
