{"moveId":"con-06-original-sin-and-atonement","type":"fallacy","label":"Equivocation","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Calling atonement a human sacrifice compresses several theological accounts and may be an uncharitable formulation, but the move does not shift the meaning of a key term within its argument. The assessed problem is oversimplification, not equivocation."}],"definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"equivocation","label":"Equivocation","definition":"Shifting the meaning of a key word or phrase during the argument, making the reasoning seem stronger than it is.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-06-original-sin-and-atonement","side":"con","sectionTitle":"Sin, freedom, and moral doctrine","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"O’Connor challenges original sin and sacrificial atonement as morally troubling doctrines, asking why an omnipotent God could not forgive humanity without sacrifice.","lockedProposition":"Original sin and sacrificial atonement may be morally grotesque ideas internal to Christianity rather than later institutional abuses.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"is the idea that everyone is born in sin the idea that there needs to be a a human sacrifice in order to atone us when God presumably could have just forgiven us anyway and he sees this as grotesque and this isn't the Crusades or the Inquisition this isn't something that the Christian church has done is something that's baked into the Christian religion and I","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: O’Connor formulates a focused internal challenge: if God possesses authority to forgive, the apparent necessity of inherited sin and sacrificial atonement requires a morally coherent explanation. Principal limitation: Describing atonement simply as human sacrifice compresses diverse substitutionary, participatory, exemplary, and restorative accounts, none of which receives serious consideration in the exchange. Live burden: Hirsi Ali must explain why forgiveness relates to Christ’s death and how responsibility remains just, while O’Connor must engage the strongest theological formulation rather than only its severest rendering."}}
{"moveId":"con-06-original-sin-and-atonement","type":"bias","label":"Belief bias","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Calling inherited sin and sacrificial atonement grotesque risks relying on intuitive moral plausibility, but the move also states a substantive internal objection about why an able God could not simply forgive; the rationale identifies compression of rival accounts, not conclusion-based evaluation."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"belief-bias","label":"Belief bias","definition":"Judging an argument by whether its conclusion feels believable instead of evaluating whether the conclusion follows from the premises.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/belief-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-06-original-sin-and-atonement","side":"con","sectionTitle":"Sin, freedom, and moral doctrine","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"O’Connor challenges original sin and sacrificial atonement as morally troubling doctrines, asking why an omnipotent God could not forgive humanity without sacrifice.","lockedProposition":"Original sin and sacrificial atonement may be morally grotesque ideas internal to Christianity rather than later institutional abuses.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"is the idea that everyone is born in sin the idea that there needs to be a a human sacrifice in order to atone us when God presumably could have just forgiven us anyway and he sees this as grotesque and this isn't the Crusades or the Inquisition this isn't something that the Christian church has done is something that's baked into the Christian religion and I","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: O’Connor formulates a focused internal challenge: if God possesses authority to forgive, the apparent necessity of inherited sin and sacrificial atonement requires a morally coherent explanation. Principal limitation: Describing atonement simply as human sacrifice compresses diverse substitutionary, participatory, exemplary, and restorative accounts, none of which receives serious consideration in the exchange. Live burden: Hirsi Ali must explain why forgiveness relates to Christ’s death and how responsibility remains just, while O’Connor must engage the strongest theological formulation rather than only its severest rendering."}}
{"moveId":"pro-13-freedom-and-forgiveness","type":"fallacy","label":"Red herring","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The move replaces the specific challenge about inherited sin, sacrificial atonement, and why Christ's death was necessary with an attractive general account of freedom and repeated forgiveness, leaving the original mechanism insufficiently answered."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The move replaces the specific challenge about inherited sin and the necessity of Christ's death with an appealing general account of freedom, moral choice, and repeated forgiveness. The assessment explicitly says this broader account leaves the disputed mechanism unanswered."}],"definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"red-herring","label":"Red herring","definition":"Diverting attention from the issue under dispute to a related but less relevant point, leaving the original challenge insufficiently answered.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/red-herring/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-13-freedom-and-forgiveness","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Sin, freedom, and moral doctrine","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali reframes Christianity around moral freedom, the coexistence of good and evil within people, and God’s repeated forgiveness after wrongful choices.","lockedProposition":"Christian teaching combines moral freedom, recognition of inner good and evil, and repeated forgiveness.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"Christian God as I see to who says you are um you know free to do as you as you wish here are the laws here's what it means to be a good person and you choose uh what you want to do about that and if I make terrible choices I'm forgiven over and over and over again and I think that that recognition that good and evil are in us and that we have the free will to choose that's a much more powerful","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali presents a coherent positive moral picture in which law informs free agency, human beings recognize their mixed capacities, and repeated forgiveness enables restoration after failure. Principal limitation: This interpretation is unsupported by textual argument and does not explain inherited sin, why Christ’s death was necessary, or how divine foreknowledge and punishment interact with freedom. Live burden: To answer O’Connor, she must connect forgiveness to atonement’s disputed mechanism rather than replace the specific objection with a broader account she finds psychologically powerful."}}
{"moveId":"con-07-celestial-dictator","type":"fallacy","label":"Appeal to authority","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"O’Connor attributes the celestial-dictator criticism to Christopher Hitchens, but the force of the move comes from the stated surveillance, thought judgment, and punishment considerations, not Hitchens's status. Its assessed weakness is missing textual support."}],"definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"appeal-to-authority","label":"Appeal to authority","definition":"Relying on a person or institution's status as if it settles the argument, especially when the relevant evidence, expertise, or consensus has not been shown.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/appeal-to-authority/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-07-celestial-dictator","side":"con","sectionTitle":"Sin, freedom, and moral doctrine","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Replying to the freedom-and-forgiveness account, O’Connor argues that divine surveillance and punishment even for private disobedience undermine its picture of Christian moral agency.","lockedProposition":"Christianity may portray God as a dictator who surveils people and punishes even private disobedience.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"the big criticism that Christopher Hitchin made in the other direction he said that God is a Celestial dictator God is somebody who is constantly surveilling you and and watching you while you sleep and convicting you of thought crime and and punishing you for even disob disobedience in your own mind","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: O’Connor directly contests the freedom-centered reply by identifying how constant observation, judgment of thought, and threatened punishment may create coercive conditions around nominal choice. Principal limitation: The vivid characterization receives no textual support and largely omits Hirsi Ali’s emphasis on repeated forgiveness, freely chosen relationship, and the possibility that knowledge of conduct differs from preventing agency. Live burden: He must show that divine oversight actually undermines morally significant freedom rather than merely attaching consequences, while she must reconcile love and forgiveness with mental judgment."}}
{"moveId":"pro-14-apologetics-and-doubt","type":"fallacy","label":"Appeal to authority","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Mentioning apologetics without naming an argument does not establish its quality, but the move invokes the literature as evidence that some Christians seriously investigate and doubt, not as authoritative status that by itself settles Christianity's truth."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The existence of apologetic literature is used as a counterexample to universal Christian closure, not as an authoritative source that settles Christianity's truth. The assessment criticizes the absence of a particular argument or safeguard, which is not itself an appeal to authority."}],"definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"appeal-to-authority","label":"Appeal to authority","definition":"Relying on a person or institution's status as if it settles the argument, especially when the relevant evidence, expertise, or consensus has not been shown.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/appeal-to-authority/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-14-apologetics-and-doubt","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Epistemic openness and dogmatism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali replies that apologetics seriously seeks to understand and explain Christian belief while accommodating substantial doubt, thereby challenging atheism’s claim to distinctive inquiry.","lockedProposition":"Christian apologetics seriously attempts to understand and explain belief while accommodating substantial doubt.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"more so if you read The Works of the Apologetics is really this whole exercise in trying to Grapple with that where they're saying we're not believing in something just because we believe in it and we're sticking to this really very serious attempts at trying to understand to explain um and there is a great deal of Doubt M and so out so I","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali provides a relevant counterexample to universal religious closure by pointing toward a substantial Christian literature explicitly organized around explanation, objections, and acknowledged doubt. Principal limitation: She identifies no particular apologist, argument, methodological safeguard, or successful response, so the existence of apologetics cannot establish its quality or representativeness among believers. Live burden: The move must show not only that Christians investigate but that their inquiry remains open to disconfirmation and produces warranted conclusions under standards comparable to those demanded elsewhere."}}
{"moveId":"con-08-atheist-justification-standard","type":"fallacy","label":"Special pleading","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The move grants atheism its ideal self-description as evidence-analyzing and belief-withholding while the comparison concerns how religious people actually behave, applying an aspirational standard to one side without a principled reason for that asymmetry."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"O’Connor presents atheism through its ideal evidential self-description while the surrounding comparison scrutinizes religious practice and closure. The assessment identifies this mismatched treatment directly: an aspirational account for his side without evidence that actual adherents follow it."}],"definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"special-pleading","label":"Special pleading","definition":"Applying an exception or different standard to one side of the argument without giving a principled reason for the difference.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/special-pleading/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-08-atheist-justification-standard","side":"con","sectionTitle":"Epistemic openness and dogmatism","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"O’Connor states the atheist ideal of withholding unjustified belief and asks for evidence that atheists exhibit the comparative closed-mindedness Hirsi Ali alleges.","lockedProposition":"The atheist ideal is to withhold unjustified belief, so comparative atheist dogmatism must be demonstrated rather than assumed.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"evidence uh and the atheist message was always we're people who analyze these questions we're people who plug in those gaps in our knowledge and if we can't justify something we're not going to believe it and and with that they Levy this this closed mind minded accusation against the religious and I I guess there's one thing to say that they do it as well do you think that they do it","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: O’Connor articulates a coherent epistemic standard—investigate uncertainty and withhold assent when justification fails—then correctly asks whether accusations of atheist closure are evidentially demonstrated. Principal limitation: He presents an aspirational account of atheism without evidence that actual atheists reliably follow it, potentially granting his community its best self-description while examining religious practice sociologically. Live burden: Hirsi Ali must offer comparative examples supporting her asymmetry, while O’Connor must acknowledge that professed evidential standards can coexist with motivated or unreflective adherence."}}
{"moveId":"pro-15-activist-atheist-certainty","type":"fallacy","label":"Special pleading","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The proposed mechanism treats atheist activism and its identity-selling incentives as especially blinding while offering no principled reason that religious advocacy escapes the same incentives, even as the move claims greater closure on the atheist side."}],"definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"special-pleading","label":"Special pleading","definition":"Applying an exception or different standard to one side of the argument without giving a principled reason for the difference.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/special-pleading/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-15-activist-atheist-certainty","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Epistemic openness and dogmatism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali qualifies her criticism to activist atheists, arguing that advocacy can harden confidence into arrogance and a conviction that nothing remains learnable.","lockedProposition":"Though not true of every atheist, activist atheism tends toward confidence and arrogance that close further inquiry.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"more close-mindedness comes from the atheist side and more certainty again not for every atheist but definitely if you take atheism and you you turn it into an activist position because then you selling something and that's what is um I mean it's blinding isn't it you become so confident and I think even um a little more than confidence there's an element of arrogance in this this is it there's really nothing more to to know or learn","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali narrows her allegation by excluding many ordinary atheists and proposes a recognizable mechanism through which identity-based advocacy may reward confidence and discourage continued learning. Principal limitation: No comparative cases or evidence establish that activism causes closure, that the tendency is stronger among atheists, or that religious advocacy avoids the same incentives. Live burden: She must answer O’Connor’s symmetry claim with representative evidence and explain why activist atheist certainty matters to the affirmative justification of Christianity rather than merely to movement culture."}}
{"moveId":"pro-15-activist-atheist-certainty","type":"bias","label":"Confirmation bias","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The move speculates that advocacy can blind activists, but it provides no evidence that Hirsi Ali selected favorable cases or discounted contrary information in reaching the claim. Unsupported causal and comparative assertions do not alone establish confirmation bias."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"confirmation-bias","label":"Confirmation bias","definition":"Seeking, interpreting, or remembering evidence in ways that favor an existing view while discounting contrary information.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/confirmation-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-15-activist-atheist-certainty","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Epistemic openness and dogmatism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali qualifies her criticism to activist atheists, arguing that advocacy can harden confidence into arrogance and a conviction that nothing remains learnable.","lockedProposition":"Though not true of every atheist, activist atheism tends toward confidence and arrogance that close further inquiry.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"more close-mindedness comes from the atheist side and more certainty again not for every atheist but definitely if you take atheism and you you turn it into an activist position because then you selling something and that's what is um I mean it's blinding isn't it you become so confident and I think even um a little more than confidence there's an element of arrogance in this this is it there's really nothing more to to know or learn","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali narrows her allegation by excluding many ordinary atheists and proposes a recognizable mechanism through which identity-based advocacy may reward confidence and discourage continued learning. Principal limitation: No comparative cases or evidence establish that activism causes closure, that the tendency is stronger among atheists, or that religious advocacy avoids the same incentives. Live burden: She must answer O’Connor’s symmetry claim with representative evidence and explain why activist atheist certainty matters to the affirmative justification of Christianity rather than merely to movement culture."}}
{"moveId":"con-09-dogmatism-on-both-sides","type":"bias","label":"Base-rate neglect","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"O'Connor moves from the vivid local sample of atheists he personally knows to a symmetrical community-level conclusion, without comparative background evidence about the prevalence or degree of reasoned and unreflective commitment on either side."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"base-rate-neglect","label":"Base-rate neglect","definition":"Underweighting general background rates or prior probabilities while focusing too heavily on a vivid specific case or local detail.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/base-rate-neglect/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-09-dogmatism-on-both-sides","side":"con","sectionTitle":"Epistemic openness and dogmatism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"O’Connor replies that reasoned inquiry and unreflective commitment occur among both atheists and Christians, so sophisticated apologetics does not establish greater Christian openness.","lockedProposition":"Reasoned inquiry and unreflective commitment occur on both sides, so apologetics does not establish greater Christian openness.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"I know more atheists who were just atheists because yeah I've never seen any evidence whatever haven't given it much thought yeah then I know atheists who've sort of reasoned their way into that conclusion I think it happens on both sides in other words but you're","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: O’Connor answers with symmetrical calibration, candidly conceding unreflective atheists while distinguishing them from people who reason toward nonbelief and extending that distinction fairly across communities. Principal limitation: His personal acquaintance offers anecdotal illustration rather than comparative evidence about prevalence, causes, or degrees of closure among either Christians or atheists. Live burden: Hirsi Ali must justify her claim of greater activist-atheist certainty, while O’Connor need only defeat the asymmetry rather than prove equal distributions of dogmatism."}}

{"moveId":"con-09-dogmatism-on-both-sides","type":"bias","label":"Subjective validation","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"O’Connor's personal acquaintances provide anecdotal evidence, but he does not treat the conclusion as accurate because it feels meaningful or confirms a private experience. The assessment flags inadequate prevalence evidence, which does not exactly instantiate subjective validation."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"subjective-validation","label":"Subjective validation","definition":"Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-09-dogmatism-on-both-sides","side":"con","sectionTitle":"Epistemic openness and dogmatism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"O’Connor replies that reasoned inquiry and unreflective commitment occur among both atheists and Christians, so sophisticated apologetics does not establish greater Christian openness.","lockedProposition":"Reasoned inquiry and unreflective commitment occur on both sides, so apologetics does not establish greater Christian openness.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"I know more atheists who were just atheists because yeah I've never seen any evidence whatever haven't given it much thought yeah then I know atheists who've sort of reasoned their way into that conclusion I think it happens on both sides in other words but you're","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: O’Connor answers with symmetrical calibration, candidly conceding unreflective atheists while distinguishing them from people who reason toward nonbelief and extending that distinction fairly across communities. Principal limitation: His personal acquaintance offers anecdotal illustration rather than comparative evidence about prevalence, causes, or degrees of closure among either Christians or atheists. Live burden: Hirsi Ali must justify her claim of greater activist-atheist certainty, while O’Connor need only defeat the asymmetry rather than prove equal distributions of dogmatism."}}
{"moveId":"pro-16-concedes-some-christian-certainty","type":"bias","label":"Subjective validation","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"After conceding Christian certainty, Hirsi Ali presents the wonderful feeling of having an answer and a loving conversational relationship with God as personal assurance, although that resonance cannot establish that the perceived divine interlocutor exists."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Hirsi Ali presents the personally wonderful experience of prayer and a loving relationship with God as her assurance that she has the answer. The assessment notes that this experiential assurance does not establish that the perceived divine interlocutor exists."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"subjective-validation","label":"Subjective validation","definition":"Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-16-concedes-some-christian-certainty","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Epistemic openness and dogmatism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali concedes that many Christians claim closed certainty, while grounding her own assurance in a loving, conversational, and prayerful relationship with God.","lockedProposition":"She concedes that many Christians claim certainty, while describing a loving, prayerful relationship with God as personal assurance.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"that's the case for a lot of Christians and I have to tell you on a personal level it is wonderful to have the answer to have this relationship with God a god who's loving and whom I talk to and pray","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali makes a substantial and well-calibrated concession that O’Connor’s description applies to many Christians, preventing her earlier criticism from becoming a categorical double standard. Principal limitation: Her loving prayer relationship explains the experiential character of assurance but does not show that the perceived interlocutor exists or that her confidence remains open to correction. Live burden: She must distinguish personally stabilizing trust from inquiry-ending certainty and provide procedures by which contrary evidence could revise her conclusion."}}
{"moveId":"con-10-christian-certainty-counterexample","type":"bias","label":"Confirmation bias","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The hypothetical Christian posture illustrates how scriptural certainty could close inquiry, but the move offers no evidence that O’Connor sought only confirming examples or discounted contrary ones. Its limitation is unproved frequency and representativeness, not demonstrated confirmation bias."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"confirmation-bias","label":"Confirmation bias","definition":"Seeking, interpreting, or remembering evidence in ways that favor an existing view while discounting contrary information.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/confirmation-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-10-christian-certainty-counterexample","side":"con","sectionTitle":"Epistemic openness and dogmatism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"O’Connor answers that Christians can likewise treat scripture as a final answer to ultimate questions and therefore see no need for further inquiry.","lockedProposition":"Christian scriptural certainty can close inquiry in the same way attributed to activist atheism.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"mean people say that that's what the what the Christians say they say I have my answer in this book and I'm I'm a humble Christian who has the answer to the to the biggest question of the universe and knows the truth and there's no need for further inquiry because we have we have the Scripture it tells us tells us the way things are say that","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: O’Connor applies Hirsi Ali’s closure mechanism symmetrically, showing how possessing a supposedly final scriptural answer can terminate inquiry just as confidently as activist atheism might. Principal limitation: The recognizable posture is presented hypothetically, without evidence establishing its frequency or distinguishing trust in scripture from total refusal to investigate interpretation and counterevidence. Live burden: Hirsi Ali must explain why her own assurance remains corrigible or retreat from comparative claims, while O’Connor must avoid generalizing this counterexample to all Christians."}}
{"moveId":"assurance-prompts-inquiry","type":"bias","label":"Confirmation bias","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The move says prior assurance intensifies efforts to prove and find the presumed something, explicitly orienting inquiry toward confirmation while supplying no procedure for seeking disconfirmation or abandoning the initial belief if contrary evidence emerges."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The proposed inquiry begins by taking the existence claim as given and becoming involved in proving it, while no comparable role is assigned to disconfirmation. The assessment expressly identifies the unresolved divide between truth-seeking and confirmation-seeking."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"confirmation-bias","label":"Confirmation bias","definition":"Seeking, interpreting, or remembering evidence in ways that favor an existing view while discounting contrary information.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/confirmation-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"assurance-prompts-inquiry","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Epistemic openness and dogmatism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali argues that accepting some transcendent reality can intensify curiosity, motivating efforts to prove, investigate, and identify what that reality is.","lockedProposition":"Assurance that something exists can increase intellectual efforts to prove, investigate, and identify it, rather than ending inquiry.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"level the when you when you take the position there is something I think you are then much more involved mentally in proving or even finding out the curiosity is very high it's finding out well if there's something what is it then and and where can I find it so I think that that opens up just a whole lot more","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali directly rejects the claim that prior belief necessarily ends inquiry by offering a plausible motivational pathway from provisional assurance to intensified investigation and curiosity. Principal limitation: Her language alternates between proving a presupposed result and finding out what is true, without addressing how confirmation-seeking will be separated from genuine attempts at disconfirmation. Live burden: She must specify standards capable of revising or abandoning the initial belief, because greater investigative activity does not by itself guarantee truth-sensitive inquiry."}}
{"moveId":"pro-17-experience-reading-and-miracles","type":"bias","label":"Belief bias","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"She chooses belief despite supplying no authentication of the miracle reports, but this excerpt does not say she judges an argument valid because its conclusion feels believable; the assessment identifies absent warrant and voluntarism rather than that defined bias."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Hirsi Ali chooses belief without stating an evidential argument, but the move does not evaluate an argument as sound because its conclusion feels believable. The assessment identifies bare voluntarism and missing warrant, which are not sufficient to establish belief bias."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"belief-bias","label":"Belief bias","definition":"Judging an argument by whether its conclusion feels believable instead of evaluating whether the conclusion follows from the premises.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/belief-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-17-experience-reading-and-miracles","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Choice, experience, and resurrection","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali acknowledges the difficulty of ancient miracle accounts but treats rarity as inherent to miracles and nevertheless chooses to believe them.","lockedProposition":"She chooses belief while acknowledging that ancient miracle accounts are extraordinary and must be interpreted from a modern standpoint.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"can't you know the stories that are told um again this is these are fast Century accounts of things that were happening and I'm using my 21st century brain to digest it and to understand it but if we say Miracles are really extraordinary things cuz if they happened all the time they wouldn't be Miracles um then I choose to believe and","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali openly acknowledges both the antiquity of the accounts and the extraordinary character of miracles, avoiding any pretense that resurrection belief resembles ordinary historical assent. Principal limitation: Defining miracles as rare explains why they would be extraordinary but supplies no reason that these reports are authentic, correctly interpreted, or preferable to natural alternatives. Live burden: O’Connor’s voluntarism objection requires an account of what produced conviction beyond decision, including historical evidence or experiential warrant capable of discriminating Christianity from rival traditions."}}
{"moveId":"pro-18-fruits-as-empirical-testament","type":"bias","label":"Base-rate neglect","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The claimed empirical testament focuses on selected Christian communities' happiness, prosperity, and connectedness while omitting comparison classes, counterexamples, historical background rates, and the mixed religious and secular inheritance of prosperous societies."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The comparison omits measurements and a defined comparison class, but no vivid specific case is weighed against an identifiable general background rate. The exact base-rate-neglect definition therefore is not materially instantiated by this broad unsupported assertion."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"base-rate-neglect","label":"Base-rate neglect","definition":"Underweighting general background rates or prior probabilities while focusing too heavily on a vivid specific case or local detail.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/base-rate-neglect/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-18-fruits-as-empirical-testament","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Choice, experience, and resurrection","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali replies that Christian-shaped communities’ greater happiness, prosperity, and connectedness provide observable social evidence, cumulatively supporting faith beyond its personal benefits.","lockedProposition":"Christian communities' comparative happiness, prosperity, and connectedness function for her as observable social support for the faith.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"that it's watching um and and for me this is empir an empirical Testament is the the communities the societies that build their lives on that faith and live by those laws tend to be happier more prosperous more connected","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali attempts to supplement private experience with publicly observable outcomes, identifying happiness, prosperity, and social connection as possible evidence for Christianity’s practical explanatory power. Principal limitation: The comparison class, measurements, causal mechanism, historical period, counterexamples, and alternative causes are all unspecified, while prosperous societies frequently reflect mixed religious and secular inheritances. Live burden: Even a demonstrated social advantage would establish utility more readily than resurrection or divine revelation, so she must defend both the empirical comparison and its truth-directed warrant."}}
{"moveId":"pro-18-fruits-as-empirical-testament","type":"bias","label":"Confirmation bias","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The move interprets selected claims of happiness, prosperity, and connection as empirical support for Christianity while leaving counterexamples and mixed religious and secular causes out of view. That selective interpretation is expressly reflected in the assessment."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"confirmation-bias","label":"Confirmation bias","definition":"Seeking, interpreting, or remembering evidence in ways that favor an existing view while discounting contrary information.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/confirmation-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-18-fruits-as-empirical-testament","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Choice, experience, and resurrection","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali replies that Christian-shaped communities’ greater happiness, prosperity, and connectedness provide observable social evidence, cumulatively supporting faith beyond its personal benefits.","lockedProposition":"Christian communities' comparative happiness, prosperity, and connectedness function for her as observable social support for the faith.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"that it's watching um and and for me this is empir an empirical Testament is the the communities the societies that build their lives on that faith and live by those laws tend to be happier more prosperous more connected","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali attempts to supplement private experience with publicly observable outcomes, identifying happiness, prosperity, and social connection as possible evidence for Christianity’s practical explanatory power. Principal limitation: The comparison class, measurements, causal mechanism, historical period, counterexamples, and alternative causes are all unspecified, while prosperous societies frequently reflect mixed religious and secular inheritances. Live burden: Even a demonstrated social advantage would establish utility more readily than resurrection or divine revelation, so she must defend both the empirical comparison and its truth-directed warrant."}}
{"moveId":"pro-19-explicit-resurrection-belief","type":"bias","label":"Belief bias","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The move passes from the resurrection story's redemptive meaning—God dying for sins so people are forgiven and can live—to factual acceptance without an evidential inference, allowing the appealing conclusion to substitute for whether the occurrence follows from evidence."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The move accepts the resurrection story while emphasizing its redemptive meaning—sin, forgiveness, and renewed life—without an inference supporting its occurrence. The assessment specifically distinguishes the story's meaning from evidence that the event happened."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"belief-bias","label":"Belief bias","definition":"Judging an argument by whether its conclusion feels believable instead of evaluating whether the conclusion follows from the premises.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/belief-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-19-explicit-resurrection-belief","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Choice, experience, and resurrection","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali directly affirms belief in the resurrection and in the redemptive account of God dying so humanity may receive forgiveness and life.","lockedProposition":"She chooses to believe the resurrection and the account of God dying for human sins.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"choose to believe in the resurrection yeah and also the the story itself and what it tells us um a God who died for our sins so that we are forgiven so that we can live and I","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali answers O’Connor’s clarification unambiguously, affirming both the physical resurrection’s doctrinal center and its redemptive meaning concerning sin, forgiveness, and renewed life. Principal limitation: The reply reiterates the Christian story and her decision to accept it without supplying historical evidence, explanatory comparison, or a warrant connecting its meaning to its occurrence. Live burden: Having established the exact proposition at issue, she must explain why choosing belief is rationally justified despite O’Connor’s distinction between desired commitment and factual conviction."}}
{"moveId":"pro-20-policy-over-rhetoric","type":"bias","label":"Belief bias","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Hirsi Ali identifies policy outcomes she regards as favorable, but she is explaining political support rather than judging an argument valid because its conclusion seems believable. Weak causal and comparative evidence for those outcomes does not instantiate belief bias."}],"definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"belief-bias","label":"Belief bias","definition":"Judging an argument by whether its conclusion feels believable instead of evaluating whether the conclusion follows from the premises.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/belief-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-20-policy-over-rhetoric","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Political and cultural coherence","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Hirsi Ali replies that despite Trump’s sometimes crazy or provocative rhetoric, the Abraham Accords and pre-pandemic economy supplied separate policy-and-results reasons for support.","lockedProposition":"Trump's administration supplied policy-and-results reasons for support, including the Abraham Accords and economy, despite rhetoric she calls crazy or provocative.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"came into government and he governed and his administration up until 2019 before the uh pandemic hit the policies were just fine I'm hugely impressed by something like the Abram Accords hugely impressed by the way the economy was humming along yeah he was holding a sesh show where he was saying things that were yeah sometimes entertaining sometimes crazy and sometimes I think also designed to provoke the media and","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hirsi Ali supplies concrete grounds for qualified political support, distinguishing administrative outcomes from personal rhetoric while candidly conceding that Trump’s statements could be crazy or deliberately provocative. Principal limitation: She provides little causal or comparative evidence for the economy, does not develop the Abraham Accords’ significance, and only partially answers why character should matter less than policy. Live burden: Her defense must explain the moral weighting that reconciles Christian humility with support for provocative leadership, not merely show that some policies were desirable."}}
